sections.s2_technical:
UnaryOperator takes one value and returns one value of the SAME type. It is `Function<T,T>` without repeating the type parameter. Used when the transformation doesn't change the type.

```java
// UnaryOperator<BigDecimal> — same as Function<BigDecimal, BigDecimal>
UnaryOperator<BigDecimal> applyInterest = balance -> balance.multiply(new BigDecimal("1.05"));
BigDecimal newBalance = applyInterest.apply(new BigDecimal("100000"));
// 100000 × 1.05 = 105000. Input: BigDecimal. Output: BigDecimal. Same type.

// Used by List.replaceAll — transforms EACH element in-place:
List<BigDecimal> balances = new ArrayList<>(List.of(
    new BigDecimal("100000"), new BigDecimal("200000")));
balances.replaceAll(applyInterest);
// [105000, 210000] — each element transformed, same type

// Static methods on UnaryOperator:
UnaryOperator<String> identity = UnaryOperator.identity();  // returns input unchanged
identity.apply("RELIANCE");  // "RELIANCE"
```

BinaryOperator takes two values of the same type and returns one. It is `BiFunction<T,T,T>`. Used in reduce, merge, and any accumulation where input and output types match.

```java
// BinaryOperator<BigDecimal> — same as BiFunction<BigDecimal, BigDecimal, BigDecimal>
BinaryOperator<BigDecimal> sum = BigDecimal::add;
BigDecimal total = sum.apply(new BigDecimal("100"), new BigDecimal("200"));  // 300

// Used by Stream.reduce — folds all elements into one:
BigDecimal totalAmount = trades.stream()
    .map(Trade::getAmount)
    .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
// reduce(identity, BinaryOperator<BigDecimal>)

// Used by Map.merge — resolves duplicate keys:
Map<String, BigDecimal> totals = new HashMap<>();
totals.merge("RELIANCE", new BigDecimal("245050"), BigDecimal::add);
totals.merge("RELIANCE", new BigDecimal("123750"), BigDecimal::add);
// merge(key, value, BinaryOperator<BigDecimal>) → 245050 + 123750 = 368800

// Static methods on BinaryOperator:
BinaryOperator<BigDecimal> minOp = BinaryOperator.minBy(Comparator.naturalOrder());
BinaryOperator<BigDecimal> maxOp = BinaryOperator.maxBy(Comparator.naturalOrder());
minOp.apply(new BigDecimal("100"), new BigDecimal("200"));  // 100
maxOp.apply(new BigDecimal("100"), new BigDecimal("200"));  // 200
```

Why specializations exist — type clarity and API readability:

```java
// WITHOUT specialization — redundant type repetition:
Function<BigDecimal, BigDecimal> applyInterest = bal -> bal.multiply(rate);
BiFunction<BigDecimal, BigDecimal, BigDecimal> addAmounts = BigDecimal::add;

// WITH specialization — cleaner:
UnaryOperator<BigDecimal> applyInterest = bal -> bal.multiply(rate);
BinaryOperator<BigDecimal> addAmounts = BigDecimal::add;

// API signatures are clearer:
// List.replaceAll(UnaryOperator<E>)    → element type doesn't change
// Stream.reduce(T identity, BinaryOperator<T>) → all same type
// If List.replaceAll took Function<E,E>, the repeated E is confusing
```

